    fullStory: [
      `Marcus Garvey was born in Jamaica in 1887, when the island was still under British colonial rule and the afterlife of slavery shaped land, labor, education, and political power. His father was a stonemason and reader, his mother worked in domestic service, and Garvey learned early that race was not only a local insult. It was a global arrangement. As a young printer and labor organizer, he traveled through Central America and later London, seeing Black workers treated as disposable across the routes of empire.`,
      `In 1914, Garvey founded the Universal Negro Improvement Association in Jamaica. The early organization was small, but the vision was huge: Black people should build institutions, control business, honor African history, and think of themselves as a world people rather than scattered minorities. When Garvey moved to the United States in 1916, Harlem gave the movement a larger stage. The Great Migration, World War I, colonial unrest, and white racial violence all made the moment combustible. Black people were moving, arguing, organizing, and asking what freedom should look like after emancipation had failed to deliver safety or power.`,
      `Garvey understood theater as politics. The UNIA used uniforms, ranks, banners, hymns, parades, and mass meetings because spectacle could do what editorials alone could not. It let ordinary Black people see themselves as part of something disciplined and immense. The Negro World carried Garveyite ideas across borders. Conventions in New York drew delegates from multiple countries. The 1920 Declaration of Rights of the Negro Peoples of the World named lynching, colonialism, unequal law, education exclusion, and labor exploitation as linked problems.`,
      `The Black Star Line was Garvey's boldest and most troubled project. It promised Black-owned ships that would connect the diaspora commercially and symbolically. The name answered the White Star Line and made maritime power part of Black imagination. But buying, repairing, and running ships required capital, technical expertise, legal discipline, and management that the company did not have. Ships were overpriced, mechanical problems multiplied, investors grew angry, and federal authorities watched closely.`,
      `J. Edgar Hoover, then a young Justice Department official, targeted Garvey as a dangerous Black radical. The federal government used informants and surveillance, then prosecuted Garvey for mail fraud involving Black Star Line promotional material. Garvey was convicted in 1923. Supporters saw the case as political repression. Critics argued that real mismanagement had harmed Black investors. Both things can be true enough to matter: the state feared Garvey's movement, and the movement's business practices left people vulnerable.`,
      `Garvey's politics also made enemies inside Black leadership. W.E.B. Du Bois, A. Philip Randolph, and other critics attacked his authoritarian style, financial claims, and separatist racial philosophy. Garvey in turn attacked them with language that could be reckless and cruel. His 1922 meeting with leaders of the Ku Klux Klan remains one of the clearest examples of his most damaging logic. Garvey believed white supremacists and Black nationalists could share a separatist rejection of integration. Many Black contemporaries saw that as a betrayal, not strategy.`,
      `After President Calvin Coolidge commuted his sentence, Garvey was deported in 1927. He kept organizing from Jamaica and later London, but the UNIA never regained its earlier American force. Still, Garveyism outlived Garvey's organizations. It shaped the Nation of Islam, Rastafari thought, Black Power, Pan-African flags and imagery, and later arguments for economic self-determination. Malcolm X grew up in a Garveyite household. Kwame Nkrumah and other anti-colonial thinkers read the wider tradition Garvey helped popularize.`,
      `Garvey should not be made smooth. He could be visionary, vain, disciplined, careless, liberating, and exclusionary. That complexity is the point. He saw that oppressed people need more than permission to enter someone else's house. They need institutions of their own. But he also showed how easily nation-building language can turn brittle when it refuses accountability. His story is not an instruction manual. It is a warning and a spark.`
    ],
    costImpact: [
      `Garvey's movement gave millions of Black people a language of dignity, but it also cost real people money, trust, and political energy when the Black Star Line faltered. Working-class supporters bought shares because the ships represented more than business. They represented a future. When the enterprise collapsed, the loss was emotional as well as financial.`,
      `Garvey himself paid with prison, deportation, surveillance, and a reputation split between admiration and distrust. His impact remains enormous because he made Black self-government, diaspora connection, and economic independence feel imaginable at mass scale. The cost is that his legacy must always be taught with both the vision and the harm in view.`
    ],
    whyItMattersToday: [
      `Garvey matters today because people still debate whether liberation comes through inclusion, separatism, ownership, coalition, state power, or some combination of all five. He refused the idea that Black people should wait to be admitted into institutions that had been built against them. That refusal still carries force.`,
      `His story also teaches the danger of symbols without governance. A flag, a ship, a uniform, or a slogan can gather people, but institutions need transparency, competence, and accountability. Black pride does not excuse weak stewardship of Black resources.`,
      `The popular version remembers Garvey as the man who said Africa mattered. The fuller version remembers a mass organizer whose imagination reached across oceans and whose contradictions still ask hard questions about leadership, nationalism, money, and power.`
    ],
