    summary: `Marsha P. Johnson is often placed at the front of Pride history, sometimes so quickly that the person disappears behind the symbol. She was a Black gender-nonconforming activist, performer, Christian, sex worker, friend, organizer, and survivor of police harassment, homelessness, poverty, psychiatric institutionalization, and anti-trans violence. She was present in the Stonewall era, co-founded Street Transvestite Action Revolutionaries with Sylvia Rivera, helped house young people who had nowhere safe to go, and later worked with ACT UP during the AIDS crisis. Her death in 1992 was mishandled by police and remains unresolved. A serious page has to hold both joy and danger: the flower crowns, the jokes, the generosity, the organizing, and the systems that treated her life as disposable until public memory needed a saint.`,
