    tags: ['exploration', 'arctic', 'science', 'labor', 'recognition'],
    summary: `Matthew Henson's Arctic story is usually told as delayed credit, and that is true, but not enough. Henson was not merely Robert Peary's assistant. He was a skilled navigator, sled driver, craftsman, hunter, translator, and field expert whose relationships with Inughuit communities helped make Arctic travel possible. The 1909 expedition that claimed to reach the North Pole remains historically debated, but Henson's expertise is not. He spent decades in the Arctic, learned languages and survival practices, built sledges, handled dogs, and moved across ice in conditions that punished arrogance. White explorers received medals and institutional recognition first. Henson returned to limited work and late honors. His page matters because exploration history often hides the people who knew how to survive the journey.`,
    timeline: [
      { date: 'Aug 8, 1866', event: 'Born in Charles County, Maryland.' },
      { date: 'Late 1870s', event: 'Is orphaned and later becomes a cabin boy on the ship Katie Hines.' },
      { date: '1887', event: 'Meets Robert E. Peary in Washington, D.C., and joins a Nicaragua survey expedition.' },
      { date: '1891-1892', event: 'Travels with Peary on a Greenland expedition.' },
      { date: '1890s-1900s', event: 'Spends years in the Arctic learning sledging, hunting, navigation, and Inuktitut.' },
      { date: 'Apr 6, 1909', event: 'Peary\'s final party claims to reach the North Pole, with Henson among the lead members.' },
      { date: '1912', event: 'Publishes A Negro Explorer at the North Pole.' },
      { date: '1913', event: 'Receives a federal appointment at the U.S. Custom House in New York.' },
      { date: '1954', event: 'Meets President Dwight D. Eisenhower at the White House.' },
      { date: 'Mar 9, 1955', event: 'Dies in New York City.' }
    ],
    fullStory: [
      `Matthew Henson was born in Maryland in 1866, a year after the 13th Amendment was ratified. His parents were freeborn sharecroppers, and he was orphaned young. As a teenager, he went to sea as a cabin boy on the Katie Hines. The ship's captain taught him reading, writing, navigation, and seamanship. That training mattered more than later public memory often admitted. Henson's exploration career began with hard-earned practical knowledge, not luck.`,
      `In Washington, D.C., Henson met Robert E. Peary, a U.S. Navy civil engineer and explorer. Peary hired him first for a survey expedition in Nicaragua. Henson proved capable in difficult field conditions, and the working relationship continued for more than two decades. In the Arctic, Henson became far more than a servant. He learned to build and repair sledges, handle dogs, hunt, travel over ice, and communicate with Inughuit people whose knowledge was indispensable.`,
      `Arctic exploration was never a simple story of one heroic man walking north. Expeditions depended on Indigenous expertise, local labor, dogs, clothing technologies, food systems, and environmental knowledge. Henson learned from Inughuit communities and earned respect for his ability to adapt. Peary himself acknowledged Henson's value, though the era's racial hierarchy shaped how recognition was distributed.`,
      `The 1909 North Pole claim remains disputed. Peary announced that his party had reached the Pole on April 6, 1909, with Henson and four Inughuit men in the final party. Later historians and navigational experts have questioned whether the expedition actually reached the precise geographic pole, citing limited records and speed claims. A careful page does not need to settle that debate beyond the evidence. What is clear is that Henson was central to the final push and to the years of Arctic work that made it possible.`,
      `Henson's own account emphasized preparation as much as arrival. He described dogs, ice pressure, cold, hunger, and judgment calls that could decide whether a party moved or died. His knowledge was practical in the most literal sense. A broken sledge, a bad lead in the ice, a misread weather shift, or a poor relationship with local guides could undo months of planning. That is why calling him an assistant understates the work.`,
      `After the expedition, Peary received fame, honors, and congressional recognition. Henson did not. Racism shaped the public response. He published A Negro Explorer at the North Pole in 1912, telling his own version of the journey. But he struggled economically and eventually received a federal job at the U.S. Custom House in New York through presidential appointment. The contrast is painful: a man celebrated abroad for reaching the top of the world had to fight for ordinary employment at home.`,
      `Recognition came late. Henson received honors from explorers' organizations, met President Eisenhower in 1954, and was later reburied with his wife at Arlington National Cemetery in 1988. Stamps, museum programs, and archives now name him more fully. Still, those honors arrived after decades in which the story centered Peary and treated Henson as supporting cast.`,
      `Henson's story also requires attention to the Inughuit men who traveled in the final party: Ooqueah, Ootah, Egingwah, and Seegloo. Their names are often omitted in simplified accounts. If the lesson is that Henson was denied credit because of race, the page must not repeat the same mistake with Indigenous expertise. Arctic survival depended on a network of knowledge, not one flag planted in ice.`,
      `Matthew Henson changes exploration history because he reveals what exploration actually is: labor, language, repair, relationship, judgment, endurance, and memory. The romance of discovery often hides the hands that made the route possible. Henson's life asks readers to look at those hands and name them.`
    ],
    costImpact: [
      `Henson paid with decades of unequal recognition. He risked his life repeatedly in the Arctic, mastered skills central to the expeditions, and still returned to a country that limited his employment and honors because he was Black. The Inughuit contributors paid a different cost: their expertise was used while their names and knowledge were often pushed to the margins of the story.`,
      `Henson's impact is a correction to exploration mythology. He shows that field science and exploration are collective, technical, and culturally dependent. His delayed recognition also helps readers see how racial hierarchy shapes who becomes "the explorer" and who becomes the helper in public memory.`
    ],
    whyItMattersToday: [
      `Henson matters today because credit is still unevenly distributed in science, exploration, and research. The person who raises the flag, signs the report, or leads the institution may not be the person whose knowledge made the work possible.`,
      `His story also encourages care with disputed claims. Whether Peary's expedition reached the exact North Pole is debated, but Henson's skill, endurance, and central role are well supported. Responsible history can hold uncertainty without erasing achievement.`,
      `Teaching Henson well means widening the frame to include Black labor, Indigenous knowledge, and the politics of recognition. That makes the story more accurate and more interesting than a simple tale of belated applause.`
    ],
